92186480388 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480388 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6e264d4-7eff-58c9-af68-052f1a966bca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480515 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480515 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480515 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480542 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1ca2ea90-b950-5f61-9fa7-e9762d8343dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in Q.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $V.$\n\nII. For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "750b7607-258b-55b2-8f3f-8eb360c8a574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480670 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in V.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(t),q(t)\\in V,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(t)+q(t)=q(t)+p(t).\n$$\n\nHence, the addition of polynomials in $V$ is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186481695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481574 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "81a6ec64-50f6-581c-9e4b-c943b6c901a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1bae5018-694a-57d0-bb47-f584f45c64aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186369901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481694 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d07ef2f-2cbf-5e7d-8015-0fbfa6b7a98e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186243957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d72023a-93a2-5692-b6e6-e90b48d23afc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592